<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="143" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>No Access List for INTERNAL users on secure records: refuse Dataverse shares to a walled systemuser, remove shares that already exist, and veto on the SPA plane — so MDA and Teams/SPA agree</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-30, session 27; defect C9 owner round 2, item 5(a) + Q4)</phase>
    <gate>blocked — requires 141 (user↔contact link), 133 (C11 provisioning order; shared file) and 139 (grant model; shared files) merged; escalation triggers (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (g) and (h) need owner answers before their branches are implemented</gate>
    <status>in-progress</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Ethical-wall enforcement on the authorization write path (POA share refusal + removal); Dataverse schema change on the deny store; tags bff-api/auth/security.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Security-critical: a wrong subject match either leaves a walled user reading a secure record (the wall fails) or silently strips a legitimate share; the removal path is destructive, and the reader change can fail closed into an org-wide outage if schema and code deploy out of order.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Modifies InternalShareEndpoints.cs, ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs, DelegationRuleFilter.cs, ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs, AccessibleRecordSetService.cs, NoAccessListReader.cs and ExternalAccessModule.cs. Shared with 133 (ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs), 139 (InternalShareEndpoints.cs, ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs, DelegationRuleFilter.cs), 149 (InternalShareEndpoints.cs, ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs), 142 (which runs after this task) and 036 (AccessibleRecordSetService.cs, which runs after this task). Sequence: 133 and 139 first, then this task; if 149 lands before this task, its child-share fan-out is a census share site here; if after, 149 must call this task's guard.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>141, 133, 139</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security, dataverse, fields, webresource, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-3 days</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Session 26 defect C9 (notes/session26-uac-defects-and-synopsis.md §C9); owner round 2 item 5(a) and Q4 (notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md:24, :33).</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Make the No Access List bind internal users on secure records, where their access comes only from explicit Dataverse shares. Refuse to create a share to a walled systemuser. Remove shares that already exist when an entry is added (or a record becomes secure, or a user↔contact link appears). Apply the same veto on the Teams/SPA systemuser plane, so the model-driven app and Teams/SPA give the same answer.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — BFF authorization write path, FR-23 deny store, Dataverse POA shares, ADR-036 scheduled jobs. Follow ADRs strictly; fail closed.</role>

  <goal>`sprk_noaccessentry` can name a systemuser subject directly. One guard answers "is this systemuser walled off this secure record?", and every BFF share write on a secure record consults it: /share-user, secure provisioning, 142's auto-share, and any other census site. An enforcer removes existing direct POA shares that a wall covers. It is invoked when an entry is saved (MDA form post-save call; task 064's add endpoint if landed) and by a scheduled reconciliation job that also catches out-of-band MDA shares. The SPA/Teams systemuser plane vetoes walled secure records. Access the wall cannot remove per-user (team-held, role-held) is reported, never silently ignored.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>WHY THIS EXISTS. The owner, round 2 item 5: do NOT lose the No Access List. Q4: "the No Access List applies to internal users too, on secure records." Today the deny store (`sprk_noaccessentry`, `src/solutions/SpaarkeCore/entities/sprk_noaccessentry/entity-schema.md:56-69`) is keyed on a contact or organization SUBJECT only. It reaches a systemuser only through the user's linked contact, and only on the Teams/SPA workforce plane:
- `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1016-1034` resolves `grantContactId` from `principal.ContactId` or an email match;
- `:1083-1094` feeds it to `ResolveDenyVetoAsync` (`:596-672`) and `ApplyVetoPipeline` (`:357-410`).
Today that systemuser-plane veto removes the WHOLE record — membership term included — on ANY record, secure or not: `ApplyVetoPipeline` slot 1 does `composed.Remove(recordId)` for every denied id (`:375-378`), before Restricted's survives-set is consulted (`:391-406`). So a linked-contact wall already hides NON-secure records from an internal user on Teams/SPA while MDA shows them — see escalation (h).
The model-driven app never consults the list. C9's fix (task 036, the flag-gated swap) makes a systemuser's Teams/SPA access equal their Dataverse access. After that the list binds an internal user nowhere, unless Dataverse itself stops granting.

WHY SHARES ARE THE LEVER. On a secure record (owned by the Secure Record BU's owner team; task 144 makes it a named, memberless team), an internal user's access comes from explicit POA shares:
- the creator share at provisioning (`ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs:726`);
- named colleagues (`:754`);
- `/share-user` (`InternalShareEndpoints.cs:201-383`, write at `:344-346`);
- task 142's auto-share for an Assigned internal user;
- OOB MDA sharing by anyone holding ShareAccess on the record. That is the creator today, and after task 139 (C4) every Collaborate/Full holder.
Refusing the share and revoking an existing one therefore makes Dataverse deny, and after C9 Teams/SPA follows. On NON-secure records internal access comes from role depth in the BU, which unsharing cannot remove. The owner's rule is scoped to secure records for exactly this reason. Non-secure internal enforcement is outside the owner's rule, not deferred.

THE READER TODAY. `NoAccessListReader` (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/NoAccessListReader.cs`):
- interface `:128-155`; select list `:180-182`;
- subject filter `BuildSubjectFilter` `:191-205` (contact OR org);
- object filters `:209-222`; `GetDeniedRecordsAsync` `:268`.
It fails closed: a faulted read denies every queried candidate. A malformed row (neither or both subjects) is logged and denies nothing (schema doc Business Rule 1, :105-112).
⚠️ Adding a column to `RowSelect` in an environment that lacks it makes every read 400 → fail-closed deny-all → a contact-plane outage (schema doc :156-176 records the same hazard for the table itself). Schema must be deployed before code.

LIVE FACT (session27:40): only 1 of 8 active interactive dev systemusers has `sprk_primarycontact`, so keying internal walls on the linked contact alone would leave 7 of 8 unwallable. Task 141 builds the reliable link (`systemuser.azureactivedirectoryobjectid` ↔ `contact.sprk_externalobjectid`). A direct systemuser subject removes the dependency for entries that name a user.

OTHER SHARE WRITERS (census to classify, not assume):
- `PlaybookSharingService.cs:321` (playbooks);
- `DirectThreadAccessService.cs:93`, `:195` (direct-message threads between two users).
Neither targets a secure root on its face. Record the classification with evidence.

WHO CAN AUTHOR A WALL. `sprk_noaccessentry` is ORGANIZATION-owned (schema doc :31-37), so any role holding its Create/Write privilege can author an entry against any subject and any object, in MDA, with no delegation check. Until now an entry only hid things at read time. This task makes an entry DESTRUCTIVE (it removes Dataverse shares), so who may author one becomes an authorization question — see escalation (g).

TWO PLUMBING FACTS THAT DECIDE WHETHER THE WIRING WORKS.
- The `/api/v1/external-access` group (`Api/ExternalAccess/ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs:123-126`) carries `DelegationRuleFilter`. The filter finds its target record by a TYPE SWITCH over the handler's arguments (`Api/ExternalAccess/DelegationRuleFilter.cs:211-281`), whose default branch DENIES; an unresolved or unknown target is a 403 by design (`:144-153`, enumeration-safe), and the rights test is caller-OBO Write (`:155-179`). An enforce request `{entryId}` has no case today, so without one the route refuses every caller.
- Share-cache invalidation keys on the CALLER's tenant claim: `ImpersonatedRootSetSource.InvalidateAsync(cache, caller, …)` (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ImpersonatedRootSetSource.cs:220-233`) uses `CacheTenantFor(caller)` (`:200-203`), which is "anonymous" for a null principal. The job has no caller, so a naive job-side invalidation removes a key nobody reads, and the walled user keeps a cached set for the cache TTL.

Related open tasks: 064 builds deny-list add/remove endpoints, keyed contact|organization only (its POML :23). 067 builds the Manage Access deny-list UI. Both predate this owner decision. 149 (C10 sharees) may mirror a secure parent's shares onto each child; those child shares are share writes this task must guard and the enforcer must remove.</background>
