  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Confirm 141 is merged and read its link resolver's contract. Confirm 133 and 139 are merged and place the provisioning guard in 133's final step order. Obtain owner answers to escalations (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (g) and (h) before implementing those branches. Record them in notes/task-143-no-access-internal-secure.md.</step>
    <step order="1">Census every BFF POA share writer (`GrantAccessAsync` / `ModifyAccessAsync` callers — currently `ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs:726,754` — these lines MOVE under task 133, so locate the sites by their GrantAccessAsync calls, not by line, `InternalShareEndpoints.cs:344,346`, `PlaybookSharingService.cs:321`, `DirectThreadAccessService.cs:93,195`, plus 142's auto-share and 149's child-share fan-out if present). Classify each as a secure-record site (guard it) or not (record the evidence). Classify against secure CHILD records too, not only roots: `DirectThreadAccessService` shares `sprk_communication` threads, and a communication filed under a secure parent is a secure child once C10 part 2 (146/149) lands. Also record, from live metadata, which security roles hold Create/Write on `sprk_noaccessentry` (input to escalation (g)).</step>
    <step order="2" name="schema (prescriptive within the step)">Create `sprk_subjectsystemuser` (Lookup → systemuser, RemoveLink) live under the Spaarke publisher in SpaarkeCore. Publish, verify with describe(), and update the schema doc: exactly one of THREE subjects; a fourth worked-example row; the deployment record.</step>
    <step order="3">Extend `NoAccessListReader`: select + filter on the systemuser subject; the malformed-row rule over three subjects; an overload or parameter for a systemuser subject that composes with the existing contact/org subjects. Keep every fail-closed path.</step>
    <step order="4">Build the guard (is systemuser U walled on secure record R?) and the enforcer (for an entry, or for a user, or for a record: find covered secure records × covered systemusers, revoke direct shares, report non-removable access).</step>
    <step order="5">Wire the guard into every secure share site from the census. Provisioning follows escalation (c). /share-user returns the 403 refusal.</step>
    <step order="6">Wire the enforcer:
- the enforce endpoint, its `DelegationRuleFilter` case, and the sprk_noaccessentry form web resource (deploy via the dataverse-deploy skill, sprk_bff_auth.js first);
- task 064's deny-list add endpoint if it has merged (otherwise flag it to the orchestrator — 064 must call the enforcer);
- the reconciliation job, covering new/re-activated entries, shares created out of band after an entry, records that became secure, and 141 links that appeared.</step>
    <step order="7">Extend the systemuser-plane read-time veto (`ResolveDenyVetoAsync` / its systemuser call at `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1087`) so a systemuser subject (and the 141-linked contact/orgs) removes SECURE records from that user's set. On NON-secure records apply the owner's answer to escalation (h) for every subject form — a systemuser-subject entry never removes a non-secure record, and a contact-sourced contribution (the linked contact's grants) stays vetoed there as today.</step>
    <step order="8">Tests per the acceptance test-scope clause; build; ArchTests; publish-size and CVE per §10.</step>
    <step order="9">MANUAL live gate on spaarkedev1 (criterion 14). Record the evidence in the notes file.</step>
    <step order="10">Update TASK-INDEX.md: this task's cell to `✅ [done]`. Record deviations in projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>(a) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — how is an internal user named on the No Access List? RECOMMENDATION: add a systemuser subject field (`sprk_subjectsystemuser`) to `sprk_noaccessentry` and match it directly. Also honour the linked-contact subject via 141, so an existing contact-keyed wall binds that person's internal identity too. Do NOT rely on the linked contact alone: only 1 of 8 dev users is linked today, and a missing link makes the wall fail OPEN. STOP before step 2 until answered.</trigger>
    <trigger>(b) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — organization-subject entries and internal users. Should an entry whose subject is organization O bind an internal user whose linked contact is an active member of O? RECOMMENDATION: yes, for consistency with FR-23's "denies every active member". Beware an entry naming the customer's OWN firm organization: it would wall every linked internal user off every secure record referencing the object. Surface that blast radius in the enforce response and in 067's confirm dialog.</trigger>
    <trigger>(c) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — a walled CREATOR provisioning a secure project whose referenced organizations put it behind their own wall. RECOMMENDATION: refuse provisioning BEFORE the ownership move (step 5), so nothing is moved and the creator gets a clear message. Walled named principals are skipped with a per-principal warning in the response. Coordinate with the C11 task (pre-validate before step 5).</trigger>
    <trigger>(d) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — access the wall cannot remove per-user: a TEAM share or team ownership held by a team the user belongs to, the Secure Record Owner role, or membership in the Secure Record BU. RECOMMENDATION: never revoke a team share (it would strip every other member). Report it as "wall not enforceable in Dataverse", surface it on the Manage Access deny row (067) and in the job result, and rely on the SPA-plane veto for Teams/SPA. The residual MDA exposure is then visible rather than silent. ⚠️ This recommendation DELIBERATELY breaks the owner's C9 plane-parity rule for exactly these cases (SPA denies, MDA admits), which is the disagreement task 036's escalation trigger names. The owner must accept that deviation explicitly (§6.5 path A, recorded in the design ADR-tensions and the PR), or choose the alternative: refuse to accept a wall for a user whose access to that record is team- or role-held until an administrator removes it.</trigger>
    <trigger>(e) Job posture and cadence. ExternalAccessReconciliationJob ships disabled/report-only for removal rules (D-2 part 3). RECOMMENDATION: ship this job ENABLED with writes ON, because it enforces a wall the owner explicitly required. The cadence bounds how long an OOB MDA share to a walled user survives; recommend every 15 minutes. Confirm both.</trigger>
    <trigger>(f) STOP if the census finds a share writer on secure records that cannot consult the guard before writing without changing its API contract. Also STOP if C10's child-record securing introduced cascading shares that the enforcer cannot revoke through the root.</trigger>
    <trigger>(g) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — who may author a wall that REMOVES shares? `sprk_noaccessentry` is Organization-owned, so any role with its Create/Write privilege can, in MDA, name any systemuser against any secure record — and after this task that removes the user's shares, which is a denial-of-access lever the author may not hold any rights over. RECOMMENDATION: the enforcer (endpoint, job and 064's add path) acts on an entry, for a given object record, only when the entry's last modifier (`modifiedby`, a person — not the BFF app user) holds Write on that record, checked app-only; otherwise it removes nothing for that record and reports "not enforced: author lacks Write on record". The read-time veto is unchanged. The alternative is to restrict Create/Write on the table to a named governance role; the step-1 live privilege list informs the choice. STOP before wiring the enforcer until answered.</trigger>
    <trigger>(h) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — the systemuser-plane veto on NON-secure records. Today a linked-contact or org entry removes the WHOLE record from an internal user's Teams/SPA set on any record (ApplyVetoPipeline slot 1, `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:375-378`), while MDA still admits them. After 036 that is a plane-parity break on every non-secure record, and Q4 scopes the internal wall to secure records. RECOMMENDATION: on a non-secure record the deny veto removes only the CONTACT-SOURCED contribution (the linked contact's grants — the contact rule still applies) and keeps the systemuser's own non-contact term (today's membership term; the Dataverse term under 036), exactly as Restricted keeps `survivesRestricted`. This loosens today's SPA behaviour for walled internal users on non-secure records, which is why it needs the owner's word, not the implementer's.</trigger>
  </escalation>

